      "provenance": {
        "notes": "Row 75. Risks recorded by the sources: box blight, box tree caterpillar (the 'box moth'), box sucker, red spider mite and scale; RHS says these 'can make box impossible to grow successfully in some areas'. Toxicity: ASPCA genus page (Buxus spp.) toxic to dogs, cats and horses (alkaloids; dogs and cats vomiting and diarrhoea); NC State species page tags it a problem for cats, dogs and horses and rates humans low severity (nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, dizziness, convulsions, respiratory failure; cell sap irritates skin); RHS 'harmful if eaten' for dogs. Severity 2 is the agent's grading. Child symptoms are the agent's reduction of the NC State list to the shared vocabulary. Watering: RHS guide says box needs a drainage and must not dry out completely, containers need watering on an ongoing basis even in winter, in the ground it needs none once established except in long dry spells; NC State says well-drained, drought tolerant, shallow-rooted. let_top_dry and 5 days are the agent's mapping and a guess. Light: RHS profile partial or full shade, guide sun or shade, NC State full sun to partial shade; bright_indirect and sunNeed part are the agent's reading of 'grows best in partial shade'. Size: RHS 4 to 8 m, guide up to 5 m unclipped, NC State 5 to 20 ft; 5 m is the agent's pick. Hardiness: RHS H6 (-20 to -15 C), NC State USDA 5a to 8b. Es and it names are machine drafts."
      },
